# OpenCode compatibility spike

Verified locally on 2026-10-14. This is the issue-1 SDK mapping for later slices.

## Installed versions and platform evidence

| Component | Version | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OpenCode CLI (`opencode-ai`, local development binary) | 1.19.0 | Installed from npm; real server, installed plugin, sessions, commands, permission denial, abort and restart exercised |
| `@opencode-ai/plugin` | 1.19.0 | Installed declarations and real plugin loading/config/tool hooks |
| `@opencode-ai/sdk` | 1.19.0 | Installed v1 declarations, requests and real server responses |
| Bun | 1.4.3 (`c6da4a4d3`) | Package installation, tests, packing and probe runtime |
| TypeScript / Biome | 5.9.3 / 2.4.6 | Strict typecheck, declarations/build and lint |

These exact versions are locked in `package.json` and `bun.lock`. Bun is also recorded in `.bun-version`. They describe the locally tested combination; they do not establish a verified cross-platform minimum.

| Platform | Result |
| --- | --- |
| macOS arm64 | PASS: 15 integration tests; packed-package real OpenCode probe with a local deterministic HTTP model; typecheck/lint/build |
| Linux | NOT RUN locally. Required `ubuntu-latest` CI job includes the same installation and smoke probe. |
| Windows | NOT RUN locally. Required `windows-latest` CI job includes the same installation and smoke probe. No platform is excluded. |
| Credentialed external model | SKIPPED: no explicit live-probe endpoint/model/opt-in supplied. The local fixture is not evidence of provider credential compatibility. |

The host's global `opencode v2.0.28` belongs to the newer API generation. It is **not** used or claimed compatible here. Tests launch `node_modules/opencode-ai/bin/opencode.exe` (also the installed filename on macOS/Linux). Do not replace this with a PATH-dependent global binary. The v2 plugin API is outside this mapping.

## Usable signatures and behavior

`src/opencode/adapter.ts` is the only SDK boundary. `src/plugin.ts` imports `Plugin` and `tool` from the plugin package and passes the host-supplied client into `createOpenCodeAdapter(client, store)`.

The supplied client's v1 calls use the following installed signatures (not v2 flattened options):

```ts
client.session.create({ query: { directory }, body: { title } })
client.session.prompt({
  path: { id: sessionId }, query: { directory },
  body: { parts: [{ type: 'text', text: prompt }], model: { providerID, modelID } }
})
client.session.messages({ path: { id: sessionId }, query: { directory } })
client.session.abort({ path: { id: sessionId }, query: { directory } })
client.session.command({
  path: { id: sessionId }, query: { directory },
  body: { command: 'ralph-status', arguments: runId }
})
client.command.list({ query: { directory } })
client.event.subscribe({ query: { directory }, signal }) // async events.stream
client.tui.showToast({ query: { directory }, body: { title, message, variant: 'info' } })
```

Responses have `{ data, error, response }`; SDK/network exceptions are also possible. A prompt result contains `{ info, parts }`. Successful dispatch requires completed assistant text with the expected session identity. The adapter fetches messages for that same session and `parentID` to check all tool calls in the turn. A later assistant response cannot erase an earlier denial or failed/invalid tool call. Evidence retains the assistant message ID; typed errors retain causes. No result is inferred from `session.idle`.

Public contracts:

- `OpenCodeAdapter.dispatchJob(JobEnvelope): Promise<JobResult>` persists job intent before creating a session, then session identity before prompting. Workspace directory is supplied on every SDK call. It never adds permission overrides.
- `cancelJob(JobEnvelope)` records cancellation before aborting; abort operation intent/result, including failure, are journaled. Creation that finishes after cancellation is aborted without prompting. Late results return the recorded outcome and cannot update a superseded attempt.
- `requestUserInput(UserInputRequest): Promise<UserInputResult>` returns a typed `unsupported` result on this supplied v1 client.
- `publishStatus(sessionId, workspace, text)` sends a read-only TUI toast. The SDK toast API is workspace-scoped, not session-scoped; the passed session ID does not change routing. Status command/tool output is the durable session-visible read path.
- `createCommandRouter(store, adapter).runJob(job)` creates/reuses the run and dispatches via the adapter. `dispatch('ralph-status', runId)` and `status(runId?)` read phase, revision, last correlated result and errors. No future orchestration commands are implemented here.

## Command registration, permissions and provenance

The plugin `config` hook assigns `config.command['ralph-status'] = { template, description }`. This is the supported configuration mechanism, not an invented registration API. Its template requests the read-only `ralph_status` tool. `command.execute.before` appends the latest durable snapshot to text parts. The tool reads the canonical store without a model or process call; the OpenCode slash-command transport itself still runs a model. This API has no supported command short-circuit/direct-response hook, so model-rendered text is not treated as authoritative state.

Permission hooks are not modified and permission replies are never auto-approved. HTTP denial, rejected permission events and denied tool parts become durable failures. The real probe uses a fixture policy with `bash: { '*': 'ask', 'echo bypass*': 'deny' }`, asks the model to call exactly the denied command, and verifies that no sentinel file is created. A hard deny can remove a tool from the model's available tools entirely; OpenCode's resulting `invalid` tool call is treated as failed, never successful. There is no direct process fallback in the adapter. Git common-directory discovery is a separate read-only `execFile('git', args)` call in the store.

The supplied v1 SDK exposes no supported question-creation endpoint. The runtime has a model-invoked question tool, but that is not a deterministic user-input contract. This slice explicitly returns `unsupported`; it does not fabricate approval or launch another service. The real probe verifies stored user/assistant message roles, but an SDK prompt also creates a `role: user` message. **Role alone cannot establish human provenance.** No assistant or API-created prompt is accepted as human approval. Later human-input work must add a separately verified interaction/provenance channel, not assume this limitation has been solved.

## Persistence and recovery boundary

Repository identity is the real path of `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`, resolved relative to the supplied workspace. Runtime storage is `<git-common-dir>/ralph/runs`, inherently ignored by Git and shared across linked worktrees. Separate repositories have different roots. No initial commit is required merely to load the plugin; jobs capture their supplied base commit. Test fixtures create their own commits.

Schema 1 stores run/job/attempt/workspace/input-revision/commit/session identities. The fsynced append-only JSONL journal is authoritative; JSON snapshots use fsync plus atomic rename. Events carry global sequence and per-run revision. Reads replay committed complete lines, so a missing/stale snapshot is recoverable; a torn final append is removed under the writer lock before the next append. Writers serialize across store instances using an exclusive directory lock. A lock abandoned by a crash is disclosed as an error and is not stolen automatically. General recovery/lock ownership and automatic resume are later slices. This implementation does not promise network-filesystem atomicity or a full power-loss durability protocol.

## Reproduce the demonstration

```sh
bun install --frozen-lockfile
bun run typecheck
bun run lint
bun test tests/integration/01-plugin.test.ts
bun run build
OPENCODE_SMOKE=1 bun run test:smoke
```

In PowerShell, use `$env:OPENCODE_SMOKE='1'; bun run test:smoke`. The CI workflow sets this environment variable without platform shell syntax.

The gated smoke test builds and packs the package, installs the tarball in an isolated Git fixture, loads the installed `dist/plugin.js` using a file URL in `opencode.json`, starts an owned temporary OpenCode server, and uses a local OpenAI-compatible SSE model. It dispatches a correlated job through the public router/adapter, checks command registration/events, invokes `/ralph-status`, checks denied execution, restarts OpenCode and reads the persisted identities, then aborts a live session and publishes a toast. The temporary server is shut down by the test; the plugin never starts a detached daemon.

Without `OPENCODE_SMOKE=1` the installed-server test is reported SKIPPED. A separate credentialed check requires all of `OPENCODE_LIVE_SMOKE=1`, `OPENCODE_SMOKE_URL` (an existing server) and `OPENCODE_SMOKE_MODEL=provider/model`; credentials remain in that server's configuration/environment. No credential values are stored by this project. `test:browser` explicitly reports SKIP until the dashboard slice supplies browser tests.

For manual installation before publication, build/pack, install the tarball in the target project's dependencies, and configure `plugin: ['file:///absolute/path/to/node_modules/opencode-ralph-plugin/dist/plugin.js']` in that project's `opencode.json`. Use an encoded `file:` URL (e.g. `pathToFileURL`) on Windows or paths with spaces. OpenCode 1.19.0 exposes `/ralph-status [run-id]`. The package also exports `/adapter`, `/store`, `/commands`, and `/types` subpaths for typed host integration. This ticket does not publish to npm.

## Completion evidence

Red/green runs covered the initial durable-store tracer, adapter tracer, plugin command registration, cancellation during session creation, denied tools hidden behind final assistant text, late empty output after cancellation, invalid tool calls, abort-denial persistence, and the public router-to-session-to-status entrypoint. Each failed before its implementation/fix and subsequently passed. Existing denial, shared-worktree identity, duplicate completion, ordered revisions and recovery behaviors received additional regression coverage. Manifest/config/CI/docs edits are non-behavior scaffolding; no artificial mirrored tests were added for those files.

Final local checks: typecheck, lint, build, all 15 integration tests and the real installed-package smoke probe pass. Credentialed live checks are skipped, not passed. Linux/Windows results remain pending CI execution; cross-platform support is not asserted until those probes run.

Primary references (installed declarations remain the exact-version authority):
[Plugins](https://opencode.ai/docs/plugins/), [SDK](https://opencode.ai/docs/sdk/), [Commands](https://opencode.ai/docs/commands/), [Permissions](https://opencode.ai/docs/permissions/).
import { type Plugin, tool } from "@opencode-ai/plugin";
import { createCommandRouter } from "./commands/router.js";
import { startDashboard } from "./dashboard/server.js";
import { createOpenCodeAdapter } from "./opencode/adapter.js";
import { openRunStore } from "./state/run-store.js";
export const RalphPlugin: Plugin = async ({ client, directory }) => {
  const store = await openRunStore(directory);
  const adapter = createOpenCodeAdapter(client, store);
  const dashboard = await startDashboard(store);
  const router = createCommandRouter(store, adapter, dashboard.url);
  return {
    dispose: () => dashboard.stop(),
    async config(config) {
      config.command ??= {};
      config.command["ralph-status"] = {
        description: "Read durable Ralph run status",
        template:
          'Call the ralph_status tool with runId "$ARGUMENTS" (omit runId if blank). Display its exact output.',
      };
    },
    tool: {
      ralph_status: tool({
        description:
          "Read persisted Ralph phase, correlated job result, and errors. This tool has no side effects.",
        args: { runId: tool.schema.string().optional() },
        async execute({ runId }) {
          return router.status(runId?.trim() || undefined);
        },
      }),
    },
    async "command.execute.before"(input, output) {
      if (input.command !== "ralph-status") return;
      const status = await router.dispatch(input.command, input.arguments);
      for (const part of output.parts) {
        if (part.type === "text")
          part.text += `\nDurable status snapshot (data):\n${status}`;
      }
    },
    async event({ event }) {
      if (event.type === "server.instance.disposed") await dashboard.stop();
      await adapter.receiveEvent(event);
    },
  };
};
import { reviewCompletedCode } from "../core/orchestrator.js";
import type { JobEnvelope, JobResult } from "../core/types.js";
import type { OpenCodeAdapter } from "../opencode/adapter.js";
import type { CodeReviewOptions } from "../review/code-review.js";
import {
  coordinateReviewRound,
  type ReviewCoordinatorOptions,
} from "../review/coordinator.js";
import {
  type FunctionalReviewOptions,
  runFunctionalReview,
} from "../review/functional-review.js";
import type { ReviewSnapshot } from "../review/types.js";
import type { RunStore } from "../state/run-store.js";
export function createCommandRouter(
  store: RunStore,
  adapter?: OpenCodeAdapter,
  dashboardUrl?: string,
) {
  return {
    reviewFeature(
      options: Omit<ReviewCoordinatorOptions, "store" | "adapter">,
    ) {
      return coordinateReviewRound({
        ...options,
        store,
        ...(adapter ? { adapter } : {}),
      });
    },
    async reviewFunctional(
      snapshot: ReviewSnapshot,
      options: Omit<FunctionalReviewOptions, "store" | "adapter">,
    ) {
      if (!adapter) throw new Error("No OpenCode adapter supplied");
      return runFunctionalReview(snapshot, { ...options, store, adapter });
    },
    async reviewCode(
      snapshot: ReviewSnapshot,
      options: Omit<CodeReviewOptions, "store" | "adapter">,
    ) {
      if (!adapter) throw new Error("No OpenCode adapter supplied");
      return reviewCompletedCode(snapshot, { ...options, store, adapter });
    },
    async runJob(job: JobEnvelope): Promise<JobResult> {
      if (!adapter)
        return {
          ...job,
          outcome: "failed",
          evidence: [],
          error: {
            code: "unsupported",
            message: "No OpenCode adapter supplied",
          },
        };
      await store.createRun(job.runId);
      return adapter.dispatchJob(job);
    },
    async status(runId?: string): Promise<string> {
      const run = runId
        ? await store.getRun(runId)
        : (await store.listRuns()).at(-1);
      return run
        ? JSON.stringify(
            {
              ...(dashboardUrl ? { dashboardUrl } : {}),
              runId: run.runId,
              revision: run.revision,
              phase: run.phase,
              lastResult: run.lastResult ?? null,
              ...(run.review ? { review: run.review } : {}),
              ...(run.reviewWorkflow
                ? { reviewWorkflow: run.reviewWorkflow }
                : {}),
              errors: run.errors,
            },
            null,
            2,
          )
        : dashboardUrl
          ? JSON.stringify({ dashboardUrl, message: "No Ralph run found." })
          : "No Ralph run found.";
    },
    async dispatch(command: string, args = "") {
      if (command !== "ralph-status")
        throw new Error(`Unknown Ralph command: ${command}`);
      return this.status(args.trim() || undefined);
    },
  };
}

